## 5. The points to decide

**(a) Where the integer comes from.** From the occupation number of a matter mode. The matter register holds
one particle at one of eight root cells (`branches.State.cells`, an integer array; `seed` admits one or two
distinct cells); n_v is 1 at the root port of the occupied cell and 0 elsewhere by that construction, and G is
declared as 1 at port 0. The Gauss flux adds a spatial reading of that number and no second integer: the
electric operator is E = p(ĉ) + U p_co + H p_h, where p(c) is the tabulated minimum-energy solution of
B p = G − n(c) (`branches._coulomb`, lines 49 to 54; `geometry.data`, lines 73 to 78), and the field register
holds only the 193 modes of ker B. A bounding surface has k_R = Bᵀ1_R, which annihilates ker B, so its flux is
k_R·p(ĉ) = G_R − n_R̂: a function of the cell label. The native compiler of the source builds each edge
observable the same way (`native_oscillators.edge_characteristic_word`, lines 189 to 196: a cell-diagonal phase
from `row['particular'][edge]` plus translations of the modes). The table is the one recomputed here: 2,304 of
2,304 values equal (D1c). The 193 modes of the field register contribute zero to every bounding-surface flux.

The flux is therefore not an independent field quantity that the dynamics keeps equal to the occupation. The
constraint is solved, not conserved: with the current coupling removed, the label moves, no mode is translated,
and Gauss's law and every local degree stay exact (F7, 48 of 48). What the 48 currents add is the transverse
part: E_after − E_before = J exactly, a unit string along the route (F3, F4; 48 of 48 here, in a formulation
where the 288 link values are independent data). So the source is the restriction to one Gauss fiber of a
link-field theory in which the flux would be independent and the same omission would break Gauss's law by a
residual of norm² 2 (F7b). In that formulation the charge would be read from the field alone and a current
would be needed to keep the constraint; in the executed source the reading of the field returns the label.
The charge is not read from the field alone.

**(b) Topological.** Their embedding was verified, on all pairs and 125 images, not on a sample (C1 to C6).
The degree was recomputed for all 768 surfaces and five transverse fields with an own Gauss solve: 3,840 of
3,840. The degree is invariant when the surface moves across an uncharged port and jumps by −1 across the
particle and by +1 across the reference (E2). The excluded harmonic flux: a divergence-free field with any real
flux t through a nonbounding torus cross-section and zero flux through every bounding surface exists (E5, E5b);
the three harmonic momenta of the source have a Gaussian law, so t is continuous. It breaks the existence of
one U(1) bundle on the punctured torus (every closed surface would need an integral flux) and it shows that the
noncompact field has no flux quantization of its own. The exclusion is a stated restriction of the domain to
bounding surfaces (`TASK.md`, `PROOF.md`, `RESULT.json` `global_torus_line_bundle_claim: false`); no local
statement uses an integrality of the harmonic sector, so nothing is hidden. The restriction is needed for the
integer itself, not only for the bundle: the admitted sector fields p(c) carry exactly 1/2 through a plane
cross-section (E3).

**(c) Split.** The clause is carried by the spectrum of the typed register and by the whole-unit transfer of
the 48 routes. The package executes a static half field and no half current. Written and run here on the actual
graph: a current that transfers half the flux leaves both typed Gauss fibers in 144 of 144 cases and gives
degree ±1/2 at the two root ports, where the clutching map fails to close (F5). Two further facts: the static
half field of the package is the mean electric field of the equal superposition (|1⟩ + |2⟩)/√2 of two sector
states, a state of the source's Hilbert space (G5), and the mean field of the admitted uniform plane has
degrees 7/8 and −1/8 (G4); the integer belongs to the joint spectral values of E, not to the mean-field record. On the executed size both signed routes of an axis end in the same
cell (F8), so the position evolves by a permutation and the law never divides the amplitude of a localized
charge between two cells; delocalization enters only through the preparation. Two charges: the package shows
nothing ("no two-particle creation implementation claimed"); the statics follow by linearity and were checked
here for the 28 two-cell sectors (G6); 2 → 1 + 1 between two regions is allowed there, as it must be.

**(d) The native meter.** The 28 experiments are 7 region sets × 2 clock settings × 1 or 2 macros. Each tape
